          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Jane Austen's Early Life</strong><br>\nJane Austen was born in 1775 in Steventon, Hampshire, England, as the seventh child and second daughter in a family of eight children. Her father was the rector of the local parish, and her family was part of the English gentry. Austen started to write in bound notebooks in her teenage years, which became the early foundation for her later novels.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Writing Style and Themes</strong><br>\nAusten's writing is celebrated for its wit, irony, and profound observation of the social customs of her time. Her novels often explore the dependence of women on marriage to secure social standing and economic security. Through her narratives, Austen critiqued the landed gentry while examining her characters' moral development.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Publishing Career</strong><br>\nAusten's first published works were \"Sense and Sensibility\" (1811) and \"Pride and Prejudice\" (1813), which were published anonymously. Her novels were well-received by readers and moderately successful financially. Her brother Henry revealed her identity, but only after her death did her works become widely acknowledged.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
