          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Austen's Approach to Romance and Realism</strong><br>\nUnlike other writers of her time, Austen focused on characters and themes relatable to everyday life. Her depiction of romance was intertwined with realism and social commentary. Her protagonists, like Elizabeth Bennet and Elinor Dashwood, were intelligent and strong-willed, facing societal pressures with pragmatism and wit.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Reception and Secret Authorship</strong><br>\nDuring her lifetime, Austen's works were published without her name, appearing under the byline \"By a Lady.\" It wasn't until after her death that Austen became widely credited with her work. Initial reviews of her novels were positive, though not always exuberant, but she did not receive widespread acclaim in her lifetime.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Education</strong><br>\nAusten never married, though her personal correspondences reveal she had romantic interests. Her education came from reading books from her father's extensive library and from attending school in Oxford and Southampton. Austen's family life, especially her relationship with her sister Cassandra, provided a stable environment for her writing.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
