          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Austen's Impact on Literature</strong><br>\nAusten's impact on English literature is profound, as her novels have remained in continuous publication. She laid the groundwork for the domestic novel and inspired a host of genres, including \"chick lit\" and the social comedy. Austen's sharp commentary and complex characters contribute to the enduring relevance of her work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Financial Situation and Later Works</strong><br>\nDespite the modest success of her novels, Austen's financial situation was never robust. She continued to live with her family and depended on them for financial support. Her later works, including \"Emma,\" \"Persuasion,\" and \"Mansfield Park,\" reflect more maturity and social insight, addressing the issues of morality, education, and the marriage market with greater sophistication.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nJane Austen died in 1817 at the age of 41, likely from Addison's disease. By the mid-19th century, her works had become widely admired, and she gained a reputation as a great English writer. Austen's novels have inspired numerous adaptations, from films and television series to modern retellings, cementing her place in both literary history and popular culture.</p>\n"
              },
